    The jitter walk is kant: incant/kantJitter (registry KantJitter; a fixture includes it) walks an action's finished chain and writes LLVM IR
        as text; one C++ door compiles the text (compileIR, callIR). The C++ new-road walk is frozen where it stands (S1-S5) and is the yardstick.
    DESIGN GOAL, standing: minimize the jitter's code, C++ above all, kant too. The door is the only C++ that touches LLVM. Before any other C++ for the
        jitter, one line on why kant cannot do it; every jitter report carries the count of C++ lines and kant lines added. (C++ 190; kant 946, at 498.)
    Helpers are named in the text, never addressed; no address is written into IR text. The engine resolves a named extern with no registration.
    An action carrying a KanT attribute (its text the function's name) runs that function in place of the chain walk, at processAction's doorBody seat.
    kant spellings that carry IR text: `$ ... $` joins with no spaces; `:` is the newline; a string's LEADING spaces are dropped, so write `_` per space.
    BUILT AND BOUGHT, the morning (seal 493): the door; step 2 (one testOP call on the chain); 2b (the KanT mark and the seat); 2c (the walk,
        kwCompile: one testOP call per top-level statement, reached by position with irAt, a go-on check after each, the ending irSeqEnd's).
    RULINGS OF THE AFTERNOON, each built and bought (seal 494):
